#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:355
msgid "Motion and physics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:357
msgid ""
"``motion`` and ``physics`` are a *record* of a connection, next to the "
"``parameters`` that make it move. Where a parameter is executable - naming "
"it in a connection places the parts - ``motion`` states what kind of joint "
"the connection is, about which axis, and between which limits, and "
"``physics`` states what a simulation needs to know about the cost of moving "
"it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:363
msgid ""
"Every property has a PartCAD name and a PartCAD unit, and the set of them is "
"closed: angles are degrees and lengths millimetres, as everywhere else in "
"PartCAD, and the rest is SI. Nothing is stored under the name of the format "
"it came from. A format that states something PartCAD has no property for "
"fails the import rather than tucking the value away, and a property PartCAD "
"holds that a target format cannot state is reported when it is exported - so "
"the gap is always visible in one direction or the other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:371
msgid ""
"``pc convert assembly -t assy`` fills both sections in from a URDF's joints "
"- see :doc:`simulation` for the mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:375
msgid "Abstract interfaces"
msgstr "Абстрактные интерфейсы"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:377
msgid ""
"Abstract interfaces can't be implemented by parts directly. They also can't "
"be used for mating with other interfaces. They are a convenience feature so "
"that a property can be implemented once but inherited multiple times by all "
"child interfaces."
msgstr ""
"Абстрактные интерфейсы не могут быть реализованы деталями напрямую. Их также "
"нельзя использовать для стыковки с другими интерфейсами. Это удобная "
"возможность, позволяющая реализовать свойство один раз, но многократно "
"наследовать его всеми дочерними интерфейсами."

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:383
msgid "Port visualization"
msgstr "Визуализация портов"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:385
msgid ""
"When a part or an assembly is rendered (in a GUI or when exported to a "
"file), the ports can be visualized. When ports are visualized, each port "
"looks like a coordinate system (3D location, direction and rotation) and, "
"optionally, as a 2D image of an alleged \"boundary\" (or \"siluette\") of "
"the port."
msgstr ""
"Когда деталь или сборка отрисовывается (в графическом интерфейсе или при "
"экспорте в файл), порты можно визуализировать. При визуализации каждый порт "
"выглядит как система координат (3D-расположение, направление и поворот) и, "
"при необходимости, как 2D-изображение предполагаемой \"границы\" (или "
"\"силуэта\") порта."

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:390
msgid ""
"It is recommended to define the port boundary at all times. Here is an "
"example how to define the port boundary using a primitive sketch:"
msgstr ""
"Рекомендуется всегда определять границу порта. Вот пример того, как "
"определить границу порта с помощью примитивного эскиза:"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:405
msgid "Here is how it will get visualized:"
msgstr "Вот как это будет визуализировано:"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:411
msgid ""
"The same two things are drawn on a rendered projection by ``pc render --with-"
"ports`` and ``--with-interfaces`` (``--with-all`` for both): a marker and a "
"name at each port, and each interface instance named once with a line out to "
"every port that belongs to it, over the port boundaries above. An assembly "
"or a :ref:`scene <scenes>` is taken at its word -- what is drawn is what it "
"says its ports are (see :ref:`assembly-ports`) -- and ``--with-internals`` "
"draws what is inside one anyway, each child's ports placed where the "
"assembly put the child, which is how a connection that did not come out as "
"intended is found. See :doc:`cli`, and :ref:`drawing-ports` for asking a "
"package to keep such a drawing checked in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:423
msgid "Port matching"
msgstr "Сопоставление портов"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:425
msgid ""
"Each port has the coordinates of the logical center of the port and the "
"direction (orientation) of the port. Whenever two ports are meant to connect "
"without any offset or angle (e.g. male and female connectors), their "
"coordinates should match and their directions should be opposite (rotated "
"180 degrees around [1, 1, 0]). The suggested convention is to use the Z-axis "
"(blue) as the main direction. Male ports should have the Z-axis pointing "
"outwards, while female ports should have the Z-axis pointing inwards."
msgstr ""
"Каждый порт имеет координаты логического центра порта и направление "
"(ориентацию) порта. Когда два порта должны соединяться без какого-либо "
"смещения или угла (например, вилка и розетка), их координаты должны "
"совпадать, а их направления должны быть противоположны (повёрнуты на 180 "
"градусов вокруг [1, 1, 0]). Предлагаемое соглашение — использовать ось Z "
"(синюю) в качестве основного направления. У «вилочных» портов ось Z должна "
"быть направлена наружу, а у «розеточных» портов ось Z должна быть направлена "
"внутрь."

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:435
msgid "Matching multiple ports"
msgstr "Сопоставление нескольких портов"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:437
msgid ""
"Sometimes there are multiple interchangeable ports within one interface. For "
"example, take a look at the NEMA-17 mounting ports:"
msgstr ""
"Иногда в пределах одного интерфейса есть несколько взаимозаменяемых портов. "
"Например, взгляните на монтажные порты NEMA-17:"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:444
msgid ""
"It is desired that any mounting port of the motor can be connected to any "
"mounting port of the bracket. That can be achieved by orienting the ports in "
"a circular direction. See how the X-axis (red) is pointing to the next port "
"clockwise (right-hand rule). If any pair of ports is aligned then all three "
"other port pairs are aligned too."
msgstr ""
"Желательно, чтобы любой монтажный порт двигателя можно было соединить с "
"любым монтажным портом кронштейна. Этого можно достичь, ориентировав порты "
"по кругу. Обратите внимание, как ось X (красная) указывает на следующий порт "
"по часовой стрелке (правило правой руки). Если выровнена любая пара портов, "
"то выровнены и все три остальные пары портов."

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:455
msgid "Interface parameters"
msgstr "Параметры интерфейса"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:457
msgid ""
"Each interface may declare parameters to allow parametrized mating (e.g. a "
"slotted hole allows for a mating at an offset within the size of the slot). "
"There is a list of predefined parameters that are easy to use:"
msgstr ""
"Каждый интерфейс может объявлять параметры, чтобы обеспечить "
"параметризованную стыковку (например, продолговатое отверстие допускает "
"стыковку со смещением в пределах размера паза). Существует список "
"предопределённых параметров, которые легко использовать:"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:461
msgid "moveX, moveY, moveZ: offset along X, Y, and Z axes"
msgstr "moveX, moveY, moveZ: смещение вдоль осей X, Y и Z"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:462
msgid "turnX, turnY, turnZ: rotation around X, Y, and Z axes"
msgstr "turnX, turnY, turnZ: поворот вокруг осей X, Y и Z"

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:474
msgid ""
"However custom parameters can be defined to use an arbitrary direction "
"vector and an arbitrary offset or rotation."
msgstr ""
"Однако можно определить пользовательские параметры для использования "
"произвольного вектора направления и произвольного смещения или поворота."

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:489
msgid ""
"When the interface is inherited or used to connect parts, the parameter "
"values get resolved and applied as inheritance or connection coordinate "
"offsets."
msgstr ""
"Когда интерфейс наследуется или используется для соединения деталей, "
"значения параметров разрешаются и применяются как смещения координат "
"наследования или соединения."

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:525
msgid "Parametric interfaces"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:527
msgid ""
"An interface can be declared once and asked for with values, exactly as a "
"part or a sketch is: the values go in ``parameters:``, and a reference names "
"them with the same ``;<name>=<value>`` suffix ``pc inspect cube;width=20`` "
"uses."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:550
msgid ""
"Every set of values is one interface, whatever order they are written in and "
"however the numbers are spelled: ``m-thru;size=4,depth=2``, ``m-thru;depth=2,"
"size=4`` and ``m-thru;depth=2.0,size=4.00`` are one object with one name. "
"That matters beyond tidiness -- an interface's name is what a mating is "
"registered under, so two objects for one set of values would be two halves "
"of a connection that never find each other."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:558
msgid "One section, two kinds of parameter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:560
msgid ""
"``parameters:`` on an interface has meant `Interface parameters`_ -- the "
"freedom of movement a *made* connection keeps -- since interfaces existed. "
"It now holds both kinds, because \"the same way as for a part\" is the point "
"and a second section would be a second thing to learn. They are told apart "
"by what each declares rather than by where it is written, and the two "
"vocabularies do not overlap: a freedom-of-movement parameter states a range "
"and an axis, a construction parameter states a value type and a default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:580
msgid ""
"An entry is a **freedom-of-movement** parameter when any of the following is "
"true, and a **construction** parameter otherwise:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:583
msgid ""
"it is one of the six predefined names -- ``moveX``, ``moveY``, ``moveZ``, "
"``turnX``, ``turnY``, ``turnZ`` (or the hyphenated spellings a ``mates:`` "
"section uses);"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:586
msgid "it is written in the short list form ``[min, max, default]``;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:587
msgid ""
"it states ``min``, ``max`` or ``dir``, or ``type: move`` / ``type: turn``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:589
msgid ""
"Every freedom-of-movement parameter PartCAD has ever accepted is caught by "
"the first or the third of those -- a custom name is *required* to state its "
"``dir`` -- so a declaration written before this existed keeps the meaning it "
"had."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:593
msgid ""
"A part's or an assembly's ``parameters:`` is not split: for a shape that "
"section has only ever meant the values it is built from."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:596
msgid ""
"An interface's own freedom of movement takes precedence over the one it "
"inherits, so an interface narrows -- or widens -- what its parent allowed by "
"naming the same parameter again. ``m-screw`` above says how far *this* screw "
"may be driven in; ``m``, which it inherits, says only that a screw may move "
"along its axis at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:604
msgid ""
"Before this, the inherited declaration won and a child's was discarded, so "
"an interface could not say anything about the freedom it was given. Packages "
"that already declare one therefore start behaving as they read: ``//pub/std/"
"metric/m`` has always said a screw may be driven in ``length - 2``, and now "
"it is. A range that runs *backwards* -- which that same expression produces "
"for the 1mm screws in its own list -- is reported and read as no movement, "
"rather than handed to a solver as an interval with nothing in it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:613
msgid "Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:615
msgid ""
"Wherever a declaration says something about the connection -- ``desc``, "
"``ports``, ``inherits``, ``implements``, ``mates``, ``alias``, "
"``parameters`` (its freedom-of-movement half), ``leadPort``, ``threadStep``, "
"``selfScrew``, ``multiConnect`` and ``motion`` -- a value may be written as "
"an expression over the interface's own values, between percent signs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:637
msgid ""
"A value that is *nothing but* an expression evaluates to the value itself, "
"which is what lets a coordinate be written as one; a value that merely "
"contains an expression gets it formatted in, which is what builds a name or "
"a description. Inside the delimiters is an ordinary arithmetic expression "
"over the object's parameters, with the usual functions available (``sqrt``, "
"``sin``, ``cos``, ``floor``, ``min``, ``max``, ``round``, ``pi``, "
"``INCH`` ...). Arithmetic, comparisons, a conditional, indexing and the "
"plain methods of a string or a number (``index``, ``split``, ``replace``, "
"``startswith`` ...) are all of it: a declaration is read whenever a package "
"is loaded -- long before anything is built and any CAD script runs -- so an "
"expression may not call anything else, reach into an object, or define one. "
"An expression that cannot be evaluated is reported by name and left standing "
"as the text it was written as, so a misspelling costs that one value rather "
"than the package."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:653
msgid ""
"``%...%`` rather than Jinja2's ``{{ ... }}``, and it is not an alternative "
"to it. ``partcad.yaml`` is rendered as a Jinja2 template *before* it is "
"parsed (see :ref:`templates`), which is one step too early for a value that "
"depends on which instance of an object is being asked for: at that point "
"there are no instances yet. The two do not collide -- Jinja2 never sees "
"``%...%``, and ``%...%`` is resolved long after Jinja2 has finished. The "
"spelling is not new either: the names in an ``inherits:`` section have been "
"written ``%moveX:value*2%`` since interfaces existed, and that form still "
"means what it meant. Writing just ``%moveX%``, with no colon, is the part "
"that is new -- the old resolver required the colon and failed without it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:665
msgid "Parametrized sketches on ports"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:667
msgid ""
"A port's sketch takes parameter values too, either in ``params:`` beside it "
"or spelled into its name (``sketch: \"m;size=%size%\"``) -- they are the "
"same thing. So one sketch draws the boundary of every size the interface "
"family has, instead of one pre-generated sketch per size:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:681
msgid ""
"A ``basic`` sketch has no script to hand its parameters to, so an expression "
"is how it reads them; a ``cadquery`` or ``build123d`` sketch gets them as "
"build parameters as usual."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:686
msgid "Parametric ports on a part"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:688
msgid ""
"The same applies to the ``ports:`` and ``implements:`` sections of a part or "
"an assembly, over that shape's own ``parameters:``. A plate that is asked "
"for by thickness implements the through-hole of that thickness:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:702
msgid ""
"Nothing else in a shape's declaration is touched: ``desc`` is prose and "
"``fileUrl`` is a URL that may be percent-encoded, and neither is an "
"expression."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:708
msgid "The same opening, drawn differently"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:710
msgid ""
"An inherited instance may restate the boundary its ports are drawn with. A "
"slotted hole *is* a through hole -- it inherits one, mates as one, and keeps "
"its port where the plain hole would have been -- and what tells them apart "
"is the outline and the freedom of movement:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:729
msgid ""
"``sketch:`` is a reference like any other, so the values go in the name. It "
"replaces the boundary of every port that instance brings in; an instance "
"that says nothing about it keeps the boundary the inherited interface draws "
"with."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:734
msgid "Interface aliases"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:736
msgid ""
"``alias:`` declares that an interface *is* another one, under a different "
"name. A bare string is the short form of the same thing, the way it is for a "
"sketch or a part:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:747
msgid ""
"The alias has the target's ports, under the target's port names -- not "
"prefixed, the way an ``inherits:`` instance name would prefix them -- it is "
"a drop-in for the target, so it mates with whatever the target mates with, "
"and what it does not declare for itself (its description, ``leadPort``, "
"``abstract``, ``motion``, ``physics``, ``threadStep`` ...) it takes from the "
"target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/config_geometry.rst:753
msgid ""
"That is what lets a package make a family parametric without withdrawing the "
"names it has published. A package that spelled out ``m3-thru-3``, ``m3-"
"thru-4``, ``m4-thru-3`` and several thousand more can declare the family "
"once and keep every one of those names as a one-line alias: a part that says "
"``implements: m3-thru-3`` goes on working, with the same ports under the "
"same names, and a part written today can say ``m-thru;size=3,depth=3`` "
"instead. See the ``//pub/std/metric/m`` package, which is exactly this."
msgstr ""

